{"name":"Ghostly Tuna","hex":"#e2e6ef","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ghostly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to ghosts or spirits.","Spooky; frightening."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ghostly"},{"term":"tuna","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several species of fish of the genus Thunnus in the family Scombridae.","The edible flesh of the tuna."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The prickly pear, a type of cactus native to Mexico in the genus Opuntia.","The fruit of the cactus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tuna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ghost","blocks":[{"text":"In folklore, a ghost is the soul or spirit of a dead person or non-human animal that is believed by some people to be able to appear to the living. In ghostlore, descriptions of ghosts vary widely, from an invisible presence to translucent or barely visible wispy shapes to realistic, lifelike forms, whether they resemble humans or animals. The deliberate attempt to contact the spirit of a deceased person is known as necromancy, or in spiritism as a séance. Other terms associated with it are apparition, haunt, haint, phantom, poltergeist, shade, specter, spirit, spook, wraith, demon, and ghoul."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/02/Hammersmith_Ghost.PNG/330px-Hammersmith_Ghost.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost","wikidata":"Q45529"},{"title":"Tuna","blocks":[{"text":"Tuna are saltwater fish belonging to the tribe Thunnini, a subgrouping of the Scombridae (mackerel) family. The Thunnini comprise 15 species across five genera, the sizes of which vary from the bullet tuna to the Atlantic bluefin tuna, which averages 2 m (6.6 ft) and is believed to live up to 50 years."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Tuna_assortment.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuna","wikidata":"Q6146274"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg/500px-NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg/500px-NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Relief from a carved funerary lekythos at Athens showing Hermes as psychopomp conducting the soul of the deceased, Myrrhine into Hades (c. 430–420 BC)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Suushi_Yurei.jpg/250px-Suushi_Yurei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Suushi_Yurei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Suushi_Yurei.jpg/250px-Suushi_Yurei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yūrei (Japanese ghost) from the Hyakkai Zukan, c. 1737","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASuushi_Yurei.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Union_Graveyard_III.jpg/500px-Union_Graveyard_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Union_Graveyard_III.jpg/1280px-Union_Graveyard_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Union_Graveyard_III.jpg/500px-Union_Graveyard_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_cont